{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"J4 CI FAILURE -> ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED (not rechecked away).\nChange 1011 earned LLM-Review +1 but Verified -1. Failing job: pytest (macos-latest, py3.13). CRUCIAL SIGNAL: ubuntu-latest py3.13 PASSED — SAME Python version, different OS — so this was never a version or logic defect but an ordering/environment dependency.\nFAILURE: tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ.py::test_new_key_in_local_produces_outbound_create_mutation — 'isinstance(m, mutation_mod.Mutation)' False for an object that IS a Mutation.\nROOT CAUSE (reproduced deterministically, NOT inferred): five test files load rebar_reconciler/mutation.py BY PATH under the shared sys.modules key 'mutation'. Three used the helper\n    sys.modules.setdefault(name, mod)   # keeps an EARLIER registration\n    ...\n    return mod                          # but returns the NEW module\nso once another file has registered 'mutation', the fixture returns a module that is NOT sys.modules['mutation'] — two live copies of the same source with two distinct Mutation classes. differ._load_mutation() resolves cache-first out of sys.modules, so the differ built objects from copy A while the test asserted against copy B. isinstance was correctly False. Whoever is collected first wins, so it is order-dependent; alphabetically test_differ.py normally wins, which is why it sat latent.\nREPRO (fails before, passes after): pytest tests/unit/.../test_differ_conflict_resolution.py tests/unit/.../test_differ.py -q  -> 2 failed, including the exact CI test.\nFIX: made the three helpers cache-first so the returned module always IS the registered one, mirroring what differ._load_mutation() already does. Verified in BOTH orders and across all five registrants (250 passed).\nATTRIBUTION, honestly: J4's own logic is innocent, but J4's two new test files perturbed macOS collection order enough to let another registrant win the race. So J4 turned a latent landmine into a hard failure, and the fix had to sit AT OR BELOW J4 in the stack for J4 to go green. Bundled it into 1011 and DISCLOSED it in the commit message rather than smuggling it; filed the full root cause as bug 2bc7-d77b-641c-42aa (unrelative-inbred-piglet), linked discovered_from 0a94, with follow-ups (single shared conftest loader instead of five copies; audit the other shared by-path keys; consider pytest-randomly so order coupling fails fast).\nI did NOT take the 'comment recheck' path. AGENTS.md sanctions recheck for a FLAKE; this was deterministic and explainable, so rechecking would have been gambling on collection order.\nAMEND HAZARD HIT AND CORRECTED: 'git commit --amend -F <file>' REGENERATED the Change-Id (the same trap that orphaned change 1008 in the previous session). Caught it by diffing trailers before pushing, restored the original I63b44a7b..., and also fixed a blank line that had split the trailer block so %(trailers) dropped rebar-ticket. Re-push updated change 1011 as patch set 2 — no orphan.\nCOLLATERAL: my J3 full-suite run was in flight when I edited those test files, so its result was no longer trustworthy. Killed it rather than reading it, and restarted. A suite whose inputs changed mid-run is not evidence."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785352915894240001,"uuid":"ee9b1ce2-2e91-43fc-b4ca-facd7af89555"}